From: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] overview: drop the GdkSurface on hide, not just unmap it

Opening the application launcher, closing it and opening it again kills the
whole shell after a variable number of cycles:

    Gdk-Message: Error 71 (Protocol error) dispatching to Wayland display.

singularity-desktop exits 1 and the session script respawns it, so users report
it as "the desktop reset" rather than a crash. Seen on labwc/wlroots; the
compositor is behaving correctly.

ROOT CAUSE

GTK keeps one wl_surface alive across hide/show. gtk4-layer-shell destroys the
zwlr_layer_surface_v1 at unmap and creates a NEW one over that SAME wl_surface
on the next open. Comparing a working cycle with a failing one inside a single
session, on the same wl_surface#72:

    working  (#77 -> #64)              failing (#64 -> #82)
    destroy()                          destroy()
    attach(nil); commit()              attach(nil); commit()
    <nothing>                          attach(wl_buffer#79); commit()   <-- stray
    get_layer_surface(#64)             get_layer_surface(#82)
    commit() -> configure OK           commit() -> ERROR 2

gtk4-layer-shell unmaps correctly (attach(nil) + commit). Roughly 0.7ms later,
immediately after wl_buffer.release arrives, GTK's GL renderer services a frame
that was already queued by the 180ms closing CSS animation. wl_surface state is
persistent, so that buffer is still current 456ms later when the next layer
surface is created; its first commit therefore carries a buffer before any
configure, and wlroots posts

    wl_display@1.error(zwlr_layer_surface_v1#82, 2,
                       "layer_surface has never been configured")

Whether that stray frame lands is a race on wl_buffer.release, which is why the
shell died after a VARIABLE number of opens rather than deterministically. It
reproduces only on GPU renderers (gl/vulkan/ngl), never on cairo, because only
those defer frames to buffer-release.

This is gtk4-layer-shell issue #94, which was closed when Hyprland changed
rather than because the client side was fixed. PR #119 addressed this exact
teardown ordering and was not merged. gtk4-layer-shell main as of 2026-07-25
still has no client-side fix, so upgrading the library does not help.

THE FIX

unrealize() after hide() drops the GdkSurface, so the next open allocates a
brand-new wl_surface that cannot carry a stale buffer. This is the same thing
gtk4-layer-shell does internally in gtk_layer_surface_remap(), which calls
gtk_widget_unrealize() followed by gtk_widget_map() -- a supported, exercised
path.

The cast is load-bearing. GtkWindow implements GtkNative, so a bare unrealize()
binds to gtk_native_unrealize(), an internal interface vfunc, NOT
gtk_widget_unrealize(). Both spellings compile; only the cast emits the right
call. Verified by inspecting the C that valac generates for each.

VERIFICATION

Measured on a Radxa Orion O6 (CIX Sky1, arm64, kernel 7.2.0-rc7, labwc), by
auditing WAYLAND_DEBUG=1 traces for the actual fatal precondition -- a surface
re-roled while still carrying a buffer -- rather than for crashes, since a race
cannot be disproved by absence:

    before: 11 layer surfaces,  4 distinct wl_surfaces,  1 dangerous re-creation
    after:  36 layer surfaces, 16 distinct wl_surfaces,  0 dangerous re-creations

Surface reuse alone is harmless and still occurs (12 reused surfaces after the
fix, no errors). Only reuse WHILE the surface carries a buffer is fatal, and
that is what this removes. Confirmed with ~30 manual launcher cycles: shell pid
unchanged, zero protocol errors.

CAVEAT

The panel, dock and notification layer surfaces hide/show through the same path
and share this exposure. They currently show zero dangerous re-creations, but a
timing change could surface the same bug there; a shared helper would be the
more complete fix.
diff --git a/src/components/overview/overview.vala b/src/components/overview/overview.vala
index ef3c3be..6c61274 100644
--- a/src/components/overview/overview.vala
+++ b/src/components/overview/overview.vala
@@ -334,6 +334,19 @@ namespace Singularity {
                     _anim_out_timer = 0;
                     main_box.remove_css_class("animating-out");
                     hide();
+                    // Drop the GdkSurface, do not merely unmap it.
+                    // GTK reuses one wl_surface across hide/show and
+                    // gtk4-layer-shell builds a fresh layer surface over
+                    // it on the next open. A frame queued by the closing
+                    // animation can land after the unmap and re-attach a
+                    // live buffer; the next open then commits that stale
+                    // buffer before any configure and the compositor kills
+                    // the client with zwlr_layer_surface_v1 error 2,
+                    // "layer_surface has never been configured".
+                    // The cast is required: GtkWindow implements GtkNative,
+                    // so a bare unrealize() binds to gtk_native_unrealize,
+                    // not gtk_widget_unrealize.
+                    ((Gtk.Widget) this).unrealize();
                     PreviewCache.get_default().clear();
                     hidden();
                     // The overview just freed its grid widgets, icon textures
